# matmul_bfp4_b.cbtable
#   The BFP4 weight contract. `ffn_gate` and `ffn_up` are Bfp4_b in the production
#   precision ledger (spec 6.4, foundry/convert/ft_tt_precision_ledger.c), so this is the
#   format the first end-to-end model run consumes for 2 of every 7 matmuls in a layer --
#   and until 2026-08-12 no kernel had ever been given one. The emitter modelled it and
#   ft_tt_op_l1_layout.h sized it; nothing had executed it.
#
#   Three CBs, three different tile sizes:
#     cb 0  Float16_b  2048 B   activations
#     cb 1  Bfp4_b      576 B   weights, straight out of the FTTW artifact
#     cb 16 Float16_b  2048 B   output
#
#   576 = 512 mantissa bytes (4 bits x 1024 elements) + 64 bytes of shared exponents,
#   which is exactly what foundry/convert/ft_bfp.c emits and what the
#   artifact stores per tile. So an upload is a byte copy, the same as Bfp8_b -- Route B
#   (spec 6.3) at the other end of its range.
#
#   The matmul reader must be built with in1_tile_bytes=576 to match. There is no
#   descriptor header on a data-movement core, so nothing checks that for you: a reader
#   built for 1088 against a 576-byte CB reads a page and three quarters of the next one
#   and the run still completes.
#
#   dst_accum_mode is 1 -- see the measurement block under the directive.
#
#   math_fidelity 4, inherited from the two tables beside it and for their measured
#   reason: this op is data-movement bound at every depth, so a 4x arithmetic difference
#   moved wall time by 0.6% and there is no throughput to trade accuracy for
#   (docs/tt_bringup_log.md 16.3). BFP4 gives the FPU FEWER mantissa bits to work with,
#   not more, so it is the last configuration that would want a cheaper multiply.

math_fidelity  4
approx         0
dst_accum_mode 1
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Flipped 0 -> 1 on 2026-08-13, measured on THIS format, beside matmul_bfp8_b.cbtable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three interleaved rounds on Tensix 44, prefetch 64, HiFi4, both arms built at once from
# one firmware build. KERNEL column of tests/test_ops.c -- kernel against the CPU fp32
# reference on the SAME packed weights, so the format's own quantisation cancels and what
# is scored is the accumulator:
#
#            shape                   Kt   PCC 0 -> 1                 max abs 0 -> 1
#            ffn_gate      32x1536   48   0.999880663 -> 0.999998611  0.0780 -> 0.00396  19.7x
#            ffn_up        32x1536   48   0.999881414 -> 0.999998620  0.0708 -> 0.00705  10.1x
#            ffn_gate narrow         48   0.999877421 -> 0.999998585  0.0528 -> 0.00385  13.7x
#
# FREE, on the same three rounds: 0.878 / 0.878 / 0.347 ms in both arms, 513.7-515.8 and
# 144.7-145.1 GFLOP/s in both, ns/pair-net 127 and 452 in both.
#
# AND IT CLOSES THE QUESTION PHASE 4 LEFT OPEN -- "BFP4 measures BETTER than BFP8 on card,
# which is backwards for the formats". It was never measuring the formats. With fp32
# accumulate every block-float shape lands on the SAME kernel-PCC floor, 0.9999986 +/-
# 2e-8, Bfp8_b and Bfp4_b alike and independent of Kt: the residual is bf16 activation and
# output rounding, which both formats share. The inversion was 16-bit accumulator error,
# which grows with K, and Bfp8's case was Kt=144 against Bfp4's Kt=48 -- exactly the
# "the tell was that the effect scaled with K" reading in DEV_NOTES 3. The FORMAT ordering
# lives in test_ops.c's END-TO-END column (against the pre-pack fp32 tensor), where it is
# and always was the expected one: Bfp8_b 0.99998, Bfp4_b 0.9939-0.9960, unmoved by the
# accumulator.
#
# The packer note this comment used to carry is answered in matmul_bfp8_b.cbtable: the
# emitted descriptors differ in `DST_ACCUM_MODE` alone, because pack_src comes from the
# OUTPUT CB and that is Float16_b.

cb  0  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb  1  Bfp4_b    32 32 16 4
cb 16  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
